# TASKDO — FRONTEND IMPLEMENTATION & UI/UX INSTRUCTIONS

## 1. ROLE AND OBJECTIVE

Act as a senior frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, design-system architect, accessibility specialist, and React application architect.

Your task is to implement the complete TaskDo frontend based on the product requirements, architecture, workflows, and storage strategy described in the provided project specification.

TaskDo is a modern, extensible task management application designed to work with multiple storage providers, support offline-first usage, and remain adaptable to future web, Android, iOS, and desktop applications.

Do not build a generic to-do list or a static dashboard mockup.

Build a cohesive, functional, responsive, production-quality user interface that communicates the application's capabilities and makes complex configuration accessible to nontechnical users.

The provided product specification is the source of truth for product behavior and architecture. These instructions define how that specification must be translated into the frontend.

## 2. CORE REQUIREMENTS

The implementation must satisfy these requirements:

- Support Arabic (AR) and English (EN) throughout the entire application.
- Arabic must be the default language for first-time users.
- Use Cairo as the primary interface font.
- Follow a modern, polished, responsive visual design.
- Use the specified color palette consistently.
- Provide functional pages, interactions, forms, navigation, and application states.
- Support the Setup Wizard for configuring application mode and storage providers.
- Separate presentation components from application logic and storage implementations.
- Support local-first usage without requiring a backend account.
- Never assume that GitHub Pages can run a custom backend.
- Remain compatible with static deployment when the selected configuration supports it.
- Keep the frontend independent of any single database provider.
- Provide accessible controls, keyboard navigation, and clear feedback.
- Avoid fake functionality, dead buttons, placeholder interactions, and misleading success messages.

## 3. TECHNOLOGY AND IMPLEMENTATION RULES

Follow the existing repository structure and installed dependencies before introducing new tools.

Use the following technology choices when creating or extending the project:

- React for the user interface.
- Vite for development and static production builds.
- TypeScript with strict type checking.
- Tailwind CSS for styling.
- shadcn/ui for reusable accessible interface primitives.
- Lucide React for consistent interface icons.
- Cairo for Arabic-first typography.
- React Router or the router already established in the repository, if routing is required.
- Dexie.js with IndexedDB for structured local persistence when the local storage adapter is implemented.

Use additional libraries only when they provide a clear benefit and integrate cleanly with the existing architecture.

Do not replace existing project dependencies, framework conventions, or working implementations without a clear technical reason.

Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies simply to implement basic UI behavior.

