Implement:

- Semantic HTML.
- Correct heading hierarchy.
- Explicit form labels.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Accessible dialog behavior.
- Appropriate ARIA attributes when necessary.
- Sufficient color contrast.
- Screen-reader-friendly status messages.
- Reduced-motion support.
- Accessible icon buttons with meaningful labels.
- Correct focus management after dialogs and navigation changes.

Ensure the application remains usable without relying exclusively on color, hover, animation, or mouse interaction.

## 13. MOTION AND MICRO-INTERACTIONS

Use subtle motion to improve clarity and perceived responsiveness.

Appropriate examples include:

- Navigation state transitions.
- Dialog entrance and exit.
- Dropdown appearance.
- Task completion feedback.
- Small hover and focus transitions.
- Progress indicators.
- Skeleton loading transitions.

Keep animations brief and restrained.

Avoid excessive motion, distracting animated backgrounds, or effects that interfere with productivity.

Respect the user's reduced-motion preference.

