## 15. CONFIGURATION AND PERSISTENCE SAFETY

Treat configuration as structured, validated application data.

Do not store server passwords, privileged database keys, or confidential credentials in public frontend bundles.

Public browser configuration is not a secret.

Do not commit real credentials into the repository.

Provide appropriate example configuration files and clear setup documentation.

When changing application configuration:

- Validate the requested combination.
- Explain incompatible options.
- Preserve the previous valid configuration until the new one is accepted.
- Avoid overwriting user data.
- Make irreversible actions explicit.
- Provide recovery guidance when appropriate.

If provider switching requires data migration, treat it as a separate workflow rather than a simple dropdown change.

## 16. GITHUB PAGES AND STATIC DEPLOYMENT

The frontend must be capable of building for static deployment when the selected application configuration supports it.

Verify the Vite production build and configure the appropriate base path for the intended GitHub Pages repository URL.

Do not assume the application is hosted at the domain root.

Verify that:

- Static assets resolve correctly.
- Fonts load correctly.
- Navigation works after deployment.
- Refreshing a route does not unexpectedly break navigation.
- The production build contains no server-only dependencies.
- No private credentials are included in generated assets.
- The default local mode does not require a remote backend.

Account for GitHub Pages limitations when choosing routing and deployment strategies.

Do not claim that the frontend can execute a backend or run PostgreSQL merely because it has been deployed to GitHub Pages.

